    "key_findings": [
      "When the distribution of firm sizes is heavy-tailed, the market portfolio exhibits an inherent lack of diversification, resulting in a persistent internal consistency factor (diversification risk) that must be priced even in infinite economies.",
      "A theoretical two-factor model containing market risk and the diversification factor (proxied by the equally-weighted portfolio minus the market portfolio) rationalizes the Fama-French size factor as a proxy for the diversification factor.",
      "The book-to-market effect naturally emerges from the model via the increasing sensitivity of value stocks to the diversification factor due to their lower market betas.",
      "Empirically, a parsimonious two-factor model (market portfolio + internal consistency factor) performs as well as the Fama-French three-factor model in explaining cross-sectional monthly returns on US stocks from 1927 to 2005."
